ompanies and cash  smugglings that exist with  normal financial tools crypto advocates  argue that the public nature of  blockchain technology not only serves as  self-regulating an anti-fraud measure  but that the global blockchain ledger  allows police to track down the stolen  cryptocurrency in ways that would not be  possible with fiat money bloomberg  columnist matt levine highlights this  inherent contradiction perfectly as  usual saying quote you might think that  a federal criminal indictment for 4.5  billion dollars of bitcoin money  laundering would be vindication of the  bitcoin is for money launderer's side  but i want to tell you no it is not what  allegedly happened here is that hacker  stole 4.5 billion of bitcoin from a  crypto exchange and stealing from  exchanges absolutely is a major function  of crypto and then they had a horrible  time laundering it they managed to  extract only a relatively small portion  of the money for actual spending and  each time they got money out the feds  were able to trace it from the hack all  the way to legitimate accounts with  their names on it matt levine goes on to  analogize if you rob a bank and steal a  sack of money and the bills are  sequentially numbered and a die pack  explodes in the sack and you drive  directly to another bank and hand them a  disdain sack and say i'd like to make a  deposit please you will totally get  arrested and you will probably get  charged with money laundering but in no  meaningful sense did you launder the  money it still has the die on it that's  what happened here and as another crypto  expert said the problem with laundering  cryptocurrency is that if you make a  mistake five years ago that's still on  the blockchain for everyone to see but i  guess one thing that we can all agree is  that whatever your take on  cryptocurrency remember that talking  about it constantly does not make you  cool  now admittedly we've all had our fun  engaging a whole bunch of schadenfreude  ove